#!/bin/sh
# Text for hyprlock's fingerprint label. hyprlock substitutes $FPRINTPROMPT and
# $FPRINTFAIL before running the label's cmd[], so $1 is the reader's live
# prompt and $2 is the last fingerprint failure (empty if none).
#
# - A failure that means hyprlock has given up on the reader for this lock
#   ("Fingerprint device disconnected", "Fingerprint auth disabled (...)")
#   becomes "Fingerprint unavailable — type password". hyprlock never clears the
#   prompt in that case, so "Scan fingerprint to unlock" would otherwise linger.
#   The usual cause isn't a real disconnect: libfprint's simulated-heat cutoff
#   stops the Goodix reader after 180 s of continuous verify, fprintd reports it
#   as verify-disconnected, and hyprlock 0.9.x never re-arms the reader, even
#   after a suspend/resume. Any lock left awake for over ~3 min ends up here.
# - "Reading" (auth:fingerprint:present_message, a finger is on the sensor)
#   gets dots cycling 1 -> 2 -> 3, so a touch visibly registers.
# - Empty means hyprlock hasn't armed the reader yet (e.g. the first ~2s after
#   waking from suspend); touches are ignored then, so say it's still waking.
# - Anything else (ready, retry, no-match) passes through unchanged.
prompt=$1
fail=$2
dots() {
    n=$(( $(date +%s%N) / 400000000 % 3 + 1 ))
    printf '%.*s' "$n" '...'
}
case $fail in
    'Fingerprint device disconnected' | 'Fingerprint auth disabled'*)
        printf '%s\n' 'Fingerprint unavailable — type password'
        exit 0
        ;;
esac
case $prompt in
    Reading) printf 'Reading%s\n' "$(dots)" ;;
    '') printf 'Waking fingerprint reader%s\n' "$(dots)" ;;
    *) printf '%s\n' "$prompt" ;;
esac
